#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U7")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U7.1 Exterior arithmetic",[Expand $(e_1+2e_2)wedge(3e_1+e_2)$ in $wedge^2R^2$.],[Expand bilinearly and eliminate repeated factors.],[Use $e_2 wedge e_1=-e_1 wedge e_2$.],[The expansion is $e_1 wedge e_2+6e_2 wedge e_1=-5e_1 wedge e_2$. The coefficient is interpreted in $R$, so it can vanish in characteristic five.],[Keep the sign and coefficient-ring interpretation.]),
 p(2,"E","U7.1 Exterior rank",[Give a basis and rank of $wedge^2R^4$.],[Choose increasing pairs of basis indices.],[There are six pairs.],[A basis is $e_1 wedge e_2,e_1 wedge e_3,e_1 wedge e_4,e_2 wedge e_3,e_2 wedge e_4,e_3 wedge e_4$. Its rank is $binom(4,2)=6$.],[Use increasing indices once each.]),
 p(3,"E","U7.2 Trace and determinant",[Find trace and determinant of $mat(2,1;0,3)$ over $ZZ$.],[Use diagonal sum and the two-by-two determinant.],[The off-diagonal term contributes no determinant product here.],[The trace is five and determinant six. Equivalently the operator acts on the top wedge by multiplying $e_1 wedge e_2$ by six.],[Connect at least one computation to the intrinsic definition.]),
 p(4,"E","U7.1 Repeated inputs",[Why is $v wedge v=0$ even in characteristic two?],[Use the definition of the quotient.],[It is an imposed alternating relation.],[The exterior power is defined by killing tensors with repeated inputs, so $v wedge v=0$ in every characteristic. In characteristic two it does not follow merely from the equation $v wedge v=-v wedge v$, which would only say twice it is zero.],[Distinguish alternating from a sign-only condition.]),
 p(5,"C","U7.2 Cyclic trace",[Prove $"tr"(A B)="tr"(B A)$ for square matrices over a commutative ring.],[Expand diagonal entries as sums.],[Interchange the two indices and commute scalar factors.],[$"tr"(A B)=sum_i sum_j a_(i j)b_(j i)$. Commutativity of the ring changes each term to $b_(j i)a_(i j)$, and interchanging indices gives $"tr"(B A)$. No division or field assumption is required.],[Identify where commutativity enters.]),
 p(6,"C","U7.2 Similarity invariance",[Prove similar matrices have equal determinant and trace.],[Write the similar matrix as $P^(-1)A P$.],[Use determinant multiplicativity and cyclicity of trace.],[$det(P^(-1)A P)=det(P)^(-1)det(A)det(P)=det(A)$. Also $"tr"(P^(-1)A P)="tr"(A P P^(-1))="tr"(A)$. The inverse determinant relation follows by taking determinants of $P P^(-1)=1$.],[Justify the inverse identities.]),
 p(7,"C","U7.1 Functorial exterior powers",[Show $wedge^n(T S)=(wedge^n T)(wedge^n S)$.],[Evaluate on a generating pure wedge.],[Both sides apply $T S$ to each input.],[On $m_1 wedge dots wedge m_n$, both sides give $T S m_1 wedge dots wedge T S m_n$. Such wedges generate the exterior power, and both maps are linear, so equality holds everywhere.],[Explain why checking generators suffices.]),
 p(8,"C","U7.2 Rank-one endomorphisms",[Let $T:M -> M$ have form $T(m)=phi(m)v$ on a finite free module. Prove $"tr"(T)=phi(v)$.],[Use a basis and its dual.],[Expand $v$ in that basis.],[For basis $e_i$ with coordinate functionals $epsilon_i$, the trace is $sum_i epsilon_i(T e_i)=sum_i phi(e_i)epsilon_i(v)$. Linearity of $phi$ and $v=sum_i epsilon_i(v)e_i$ identify this with $phi(v)$.],[Show that the result does not depend on the chosen basis.]),
 p(9,"C","U7.1 Vanishing top powers",[Prove $wedge^n R^r=0$ for $n>r$.],[Expand in the standard basis.],[Every $n$-tuple of basis indices repeats an index.],[Multilinearity expresses each wedge as a linear combination of wedges of standard basis elements. With more slots than available indices, each term repeats an index and is zero by alternation. Hence every generator and thus every element vanishes.],[Use the pigeonhole step explicitly.]),
 p(10,"A","U7.2 Dual bases",[For a finite projective module, prove the trace formula is independent of the chosen dual basis.],[Expand one basis family using the other.],[Use the reconstruction identity to collapse the double sum.],[For dual families $(m_i,phi_i)$ and $(n_j,psi_j)$, expand $m_i=sum_j psi_j(m_i)n_j$. The first trace becomes $sum_(i,j)psi_j(m_i)phi_i(T n_j)$. For fixed $j$, the reconstruction $T n_j=sum_i phi_i(T n_j)m_i$ makes the inner sum equal $psi_j(T n_j)$. Summing gives the second trace formula.],[Use only dual-basis identities, not an assumption that either family is linearly independent.]),
 p(11,"A","U7.1 Exterior direct-sum decomposition",[For finite free modules $M,N$, prove $wedge^2(M oplus N) iso wedge^2M oplus(M otimes N)oplus wedge^2N$.],[Use bases grouped by the two summands.],[Sorted pairs are of three types: both in $M$, one in each, or both in $N$.],[Choose bases $e_i,f_j$ for the two summands. The exterior basis on their concatenation splits into $e_i wedge e_k$ with $i<k$, $e_i wedge f_j$, and $f_j wedge f_l$ with $j<l$. These match bases of the three displayed modules. The map is induced by their inclusions, with the middle term $m otimes n|->(m,0)wedge(0,n)$. Thus it is independent of the chosen bases, whose role was to prove bijectivity.],[Define the canonical map and use the basis decomposition to establish it is an isomorphism.]),
 p(12,"A","U7.2 Finite-order operators",[Let $T^5=1$ on a finite-dimensional vector space. Show $det(T)^5=1$, and give a reason this does not force $T$ to be the identity.],[Apply determinant to the power equation.],[A nontrivial fifth-root scalar operator is a counterexample over $CC$.],[Multiplicativity gives $det(T)^5=det(T^5)=1$. Over $CC$, multiplication by a primitive fifth root of unity on a one-dimensional space is nonidentity but its fifth power is identity. Even determinant one would not force a higher-dimensional operator to be identity, for example a diagonal matrix with reciprocal nontrivial eigenvalues.],[Separate the scalar determinant constraint from the full operator.]),
)
